Thanks for the clear answers.

Here is snippet of my `infobox`, I am not sure it is the right way but it does work, if it can help someone.

```js
<!-- require infobox.js -->

var search = instantsearch({
  appId: xxx,
  apiKey: xxx,
  indexName: xxx
});

var o = new InfoBox({/*options*/});

var map = instantsearch.widgets.geoSearch({
  container: '#map',
  googleReference: window.google,
  customHTMLMarker: {
    template:  function(e) {
      return '<div>' + e.title + '</div>'
    },
    events: {
      click: function(e) {
        o.getMap() && o.close(), o.setContent('<div>' + e.item.title + '</div>'), o.open(e.map, e.marker)
      }
    }
  },
});

search.addWidget(map);
search.start();
```


**Question about Places with GeoSearch:**
I am now trying to display an _Algolia Places_ [search input](https://community.algolia.com/instantsearch.js/v2/dev-novel/?selectedStory=GeoSearch.with%20position%20from%20Places) to my `geoSearch` widget. I gathered that the _Places_ script is already included in `instantsearch.js`, so I don't need to reference it.

But I am having hard times to understand how to implement it, could someone give me a simple example? 

_It could be nice if the [examples](https://community.algolia.com/instantsearch.js/v2/dev-novel) had a little snippet to copy._

@bobylito @samouss, yes I've updated to the most current version. I have a codepen here if you have time to take a look: https://codepen.io/vaughndtaylor/pen/2431919cc9a8547ab36c019add338392/?editors=1010

Basically, the idea is to have a map that is populated with the locations each provider works at. Some providers have 1 location, some have multiple, so _geoloc is an array in my data set.

You can see the _geoloc values if you uncomment lines 90-95

Hi @vaughn-taylor thanks for the example it helps a lot to understand the issue. The first problem that cause the map to not showing up is that Google Maps requires to set a height on the element where the map will be attached. We don't provide a default value for this because it can be hard to override. By adding this line of CSS the map should be displayed.

```css
#map {
  height: 350px;
}
```

But it will not display the results because the current widget doesn't support multiple locations for the `_geoloc` attribute. This is a limitation for now, I've opened [an issue](https://github.com/algolia/instantsearch.js/issues/2838) about that. A possible solution is to duplicate the records where you have multiple locations in order to have a single location for each of you records. Then you can use the `distinct` attribute to deduplicate your results. You can find more details about that [in our documentation](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/ranking/distinct). With this data structure you will be able to use the Geo Search widget.
